<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oeantheia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oeantheia was an ancient Greek town of Ozolian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/locris">Locris</a> on the northern shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-corinth">Gulf of Corinth</a>, usually identified with modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galaxidi">Galaxidi</a>. With neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaleion">Chaleion</a> it concluded a 5th-century BCE agreement, inscribed on a bronze tablet, regulating reprisals and the rights of foreigners between the two towns, one of the earliest surviving documents of Greek inter-state law.</p></div>
